  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Adding Same-Frequency Cosines",
    "lesson_core_move": "Add same-frequency cosines by recognizing their common angular frequency, converting each cosine to its phasor, and summing the phasors as complex quantities.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Adding Same-Frequency Cosines",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Adding Same-Frequency Cosines",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When two cosine terms share the same angular frequency, do not add them directly in the time domain. Treat the shared $\\omega$ as the cue that the terms belong to one phasor-addition problem, then convert each cosine to a phasor and add the phasors as complex quantities.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the base case $A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\cos(\\omega t)$. The shared $\\omega$ tells you that both terms use the same frequency tag, so each cosine becomes a zero-phase phasor before anything is added.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\cos(\\omega t)\\rightarrow A\\angle 0 + B\\angle 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same rule works for phase-shifted cosines such as $A_1\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi_1)+A_2\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi_2)$. Keep the common $\\omega$ outside the phasor value, and convert the amplitudes and phases into $A_1\\angle\\phi_1$ and $A_2\\angle\\phi_2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_1\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi_1)+A_2\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi_2)\\rightarrow A_1\\angle\\phi_1 + A_2\\angle\\phi_2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "After conversion, add the phasors as ordinary complex quantities. The frequency tag $\\omega$ does not get added; it is only the shared label that tells you the cosines are comparable at the same frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This step is just the setup: recognize the shared $\\omega$, rewrite each cosine as $A\\angle\\phi$, and write the complex sum cleanly. Later steps will make the phasor sum more explicit, but this lesson starts with the recognition-and-setup move.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Adding Same-Frequency Cosines",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing a Common Angular Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "In $4\\cos(5t+\\pi/6)+7\\cos(5t-\\pi/3)$, identify the shared angular frequency and decide whether the terms can be combined as same-frequency phasors.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the coefficient of $t$ in each cosine argument. The first term has $5t+\\pi/6$ and the second term has $5t-\\pi/3$, so both cosines carry the same angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because both terms use the same $\\omega$, the pair is eligible for phasor addition. The amplitudes $4$ and $7$ and the phases $\\pi/6$ and $-\\pi/3$ do not change that decision.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "State the result explicitly: the shared angular frequency is $\\omega=5$, so these two cosines can be treated as one phasor-addition problem.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This recognition step does not convert anything yet; it only confirms that the later phasor conversion is valid because the frequency is common.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Adding Same-Frequency Cosines",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Converting Each Cosine to a Phasor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $4\\cos(5t+\\pi/6)+7\\cos(5t-\\pi/3)$ into a phasor-sum expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the two cosines already share $\\omega=5$, this step is just a notation conversion. Read the first term's amplitude and phase as $4$ and $\\pi/6$, then read the second term's amplitude and phase as $7$ and $-\\pi/3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the first cosine as $4\\angle\\pi/6$. The amplitude stays with the phasor, and the phase becomes the angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the second cosine as $7\\angle-\\pi/3$ and keep the shared frequency tag $5$ outside the phasors. That keeps the notation frequency-preserving.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phasor-sum form is $4\\angle\\pi/6 + 7\\angle-\\pi/3$. Nothing has been added yet; this step only converts each cosine cleanly so the next step can combine the phasors.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Adding Same-Frequency Cosines",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Forming the Resultant Phasor Sum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Form the resultant phasor sum for $5\\cos(3t)+5\\cos(3t-\\pi/2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shared angular frequency $\\omega=3$, so the two terms can be handled as one phasor-addition problem. The converted phasors are already visible from the cosine data: the first term has amplitude $5$ and phase $0$, and the second term has amplitude $5$ and phase $-\\pi/2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5\\angle 0 + 5\\angle(-\\pi/2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the result as the phasor sum $5\\angle 0 + 5\\angle(-\\pi/2)$. The plus sign is ordinary complex addition, so the two phasors are combined directly instead of being turned back into time-domain cosines.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The resultant expression stays in phasor form for now. That keeps the shared frequency $3$ attached to the sum and leaves rectangular or polar simplification for the next step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Adding Same-Frequency Cosines",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Adding More Than Two Same-Frequency Cosine Phasors",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $2\\cos(7t)+3\\cos(7t+\\pi/4)+4\\cos(7t-\\pi/3)$ into one phasor-sum expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shared angular frequency $\\omega=7$ in all three cosine terms. That means the whole expression is one phasor-addition problem, and each cosine should be carried into phasor form without dropping any term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Convert the zero-phase term to $2\\angle 0$, the positive-phase term to $3\\angle\\pi/4$, and the negative-phase term to $4\\angle-\\pi/3$. Each amplitude stays attached to its own phasor, and the shared frequency label remains $7$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2\\angle 0 + 3\\angle(\\pi/4) + 4\\angle(-\\pi/3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the full phasor-sum expression as $2\\angle 0 + 3\\angle(\\pi/4) + 4\\angle(-\\pi/3)$. The plus signs mean ordinary complex addition, and the expression stays in phasor form rather than being collapsed into rectangular coordinates yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result shows the rule scales unchanged from two terms to three: keep the common $\\omega$, convert every cosine to its phasor, and add all of them together as a single complex sum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
